<h2>What buyers still want to know</h2>
</div>
<div>
<div class="faq-list"><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is Kobo better than Kindle?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Not always. Kobo is the safer shared Canada/U.S. decision because of library access and lower ecosystem lock-in. Kindle is stronger for U.S. Amazon-first readers.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Should I buy an e-reader with colour?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Only if colour is part of your actual reading: comics, kids books, illustrated nonfiction, cookbooks, magazines, or annotation. For novels, black-and-white is usually the better buy.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is the cheapest Kindle good enough?</summary><p class="faq-answer">It can be, but it is easier to outgrow. Missing waterproofing and warm light matters more over time than it seems on day one.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Is an e-reader worth it if I already have a tablet?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Yes, if you read books regularly. E Ink is calmer, easier on battery life, and less distracting than a tablet. If you mainly want apps, video, web browsing, or colour-rich content, a tablet may fit better.</p></details><details class="faq-item" open=""><summary>Can I use library books on an e-reader?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Yes, but the country and device matter. Kobo is the safer library-first choice across Canada and the U.S.; Kindle's familiar Libby workflow is a U.S.-only advantage.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Should I buy a kids e-reader or a regular one?</summary><p class="faq-answer">For a child-specific purchase, a kids bundle usually makes sense because the case, warranty, content layer, and parental tools solve real parent problems. For a shared family device, choose based on ecosystem and library use first.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>Do I need a large-screen e-reader?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Usually not. Large-screen note devices make sense for PDFs, coursework, or handwriting. For ordinary books, a smaller dedicated e-reader is simpler and easier to hold.</p></details><details class="faq-item"><summary>What model-name traps should I watch for?</summary><p class="faq-answer">Do not confuse Kobo Clara BW with Clara Colour, Kindle Paperwhite with Paperwhite Kids or Signature Edition, or Kobo Libra Colour with older Libra models, cases, stylus-only pages, or renewed units.</p></details>
</div>
